N1  - <p>BOINC project paper: listed on the Ramanujan Machine project publications page (ramanujanmachine.com/publications); reports the BOINC Distributed Factorial Reduction search live since October 2021. Paper text (Sec. 1.2 / Fig. 3, Distributed Factorial Reduction algorithm) states: "The distribution of the computing chunks is done using the Berkeley Open Infrastructure for Network Computing (BOINC)"; "Each chunk is computed by off-site worker computers donated by volunteers"; "We executed the distributed algorithm first on a Technion computing cluster (Zeus), and then distributed it on individual computers with the help of the BOINC community. The thousands of new formulas discovered in this project are in large part thanks to the contribution of the BOINC community"; "our Distributed Factorial Reduction algorithm has been running online since October 2021. At the time of writing, the algorithm is distributed over >5000 computers, and has been supported by >1000 volunteers"; Appendix C.2: families "manually generalized based on examples discovered by the BOINC community, executing the Distributed Factorial Reduction algorithm." Acknowledgements: "We are grateful to the volunteers in the BOINC community whose contribution made this discovery possible." Reference [44]: "Ramanujan Machine on BOINC (2023)." Preprint: arXiv:2308.11829.</p>
